response.value:
Thank you for providing the full script. The problem is that your `renderTable` function renders to the element with id `table-container`, but your HTML has two separate containers for tables:

- `<div id="study-table-container"></div>` (used by study search)
- `<div id="table-container"></div>` (used by Fetch All Terms and Fetch Term Details)

However, only the `study-table-container` is visible in your layout, because the `table-container` is not placed in the visible part of your HTML. So, when you call `renderTable()`, the table is rendered, but you can't see it.

**How to fix:**  
Replace `<div id="table-container"></div>` with `<div id="study-table-container"></div>` in your `renderTable` function, so all results use the same visible container.

---

### Solution

In your `renderTable` function, change:
```js
const container = document.getElementById('table-container');
const pagination = document.getElementById('table-pagination');
const controls = document.getElementById('table-controls');
const totalDiv = document.getElementById('total-results');
```
to:
```js
const container = document.getElementById('study-table-container');
const pagination = document.getElementById('study-pagination');
const controls = document.getElementById('table-controls');
const totalDiv = document.getElementById('total-results');
```

This will make Fetch All Terms and Fetch Term Details results appear in the same place as the study search results.

---

